    fix(cascade): follow and rewrite every Cargo declaration of a dependency, not only the first (#223)

    A dependency declared in more than one table, such as `[dependencies]` and a `[target.<cfg>.dependencies]` table, was one edge: the first declaration was reported and a rewrite reached only declarations identical to it. A second declaration with a different constraint kept a stale pin after the package it names was released, which can break the workspace or publish stale metadata.

    Each declaration is now its own edge. A `DepLocator` (the section and the optional target cfg) goes from extraction through the graph edge, the rewrite key, the snapshot and the apply step to the Cargo writer, which rewrites exactly the declaration the locator names and keeps its formatting and comments. An inherited `workspace = true` declaration is still one rewrite of the workspace dependency, whichever member table names it. A locator that no longer matches fails with DependencyNotFound instead of skipping. npm and Python pass the default locator, so their behavior is unchanged.

    One existing behavior changes by design: a target declaration whose range still covers the new version now has its lower bound raised, as a plain declaration does.

    This changes public API: `DependencyEntry` and `DepEdge` gain `locator`, `RewriteKey` gains `locator`, and `Manifest::update_dependency_spec` takes the locator. Python requirements declared twice with different constraints still follow only their first declaration; that is recorded in the changeset and at the writer.

